  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Lichen Agaric is a small, delicate fungus belonging to the Waxcaps and Allies family, characterized by its pale coloring and thin flesh. Despite its common name, it has a unique symbiotic relationship with lichens rather than being a true lichen itself.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Texture",
      "description": "Small, grayish to whitish cap that is smooth and often translucent or waxy in appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Attachment",
      "description": "Gills are decurrent (running down the stem) and waxy in texture, typical of the Waxcap family",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Extremely small fruiting bodies, typically less than 1 cm in diameter, making it easily overlooked",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in acidic, mossy areas and heathlands throughout New England, typically fruiting in moist conditions among bryophytes and lichen-rich substrates.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus has evolved a remarkable symbiotic relationship with lichens, actually growing from lichen thalli and depending on them for survival, making it a fascinating example of fungal-algal cohabitation.",
